WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:09.500
What has come to be called the Hindu tradition

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.630
is a rich fabric of civilization, interweaving numerous threads and hues

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:16.030
of religious life.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:20.960
The intricate blend of Aryan, Dravidian, and various tribal cultures

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.270
has resulted in a rich variety of religious practices,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:29.380
a diverse region of many peoples and languages, and various forms of worship

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.820
through the many manifestations and images of the divine.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.960
This dynamic network of religious currents

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.950
is therefore better represented in the plural as Hindu traditions.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.950
Formulations and reformulations of these currents

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.950
have continued through the centuries.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.820
And they continue today as Hindus in communities throughout the world

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.120
reshape traditions in the context of their contemporary societies.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.780
While Hindu currents of thought and practices

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.580
have flourished on the Indian subcontinent for at least

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:03.930
three millennia, the concept of Hinduism as a world religion,

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.820
as a unitary package of beliefs and rituals

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.110
akin to Christianity, or Islam, or Buddhism,

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.890
emerged only in the 19th century colonial

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:17.330
context via processes much debated in scholarship over the past three

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:18.200
decades.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.290
While there are many divine figures, each with many names and forms,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.430
a large number of Hindu worshippers would

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:28.000
insist that this many-ness must be understood in relation

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
to a radical one-ness, expressed through the concept of Brahman,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.910
understood as the reality that transcends all personal names.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:41.610
This one reality, call it Brahman the divine or the real,

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:46.310
can be perceived in and through an infinite number of names and forms.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:50.460
The earliest literary sources of the Hindu tradition are the vedas,

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.860
literally knowledge, a body of ancient hymns and chants composed

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.940
in the second millennium BCE, sometimes referred to as shruti,

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.110
meaning literally what is heard.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:07.320
The vedas were not read on the page, but recited orally in metered verse.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.039
The Upanishads consider the end of vedas and dating

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:14.620
largely from the eighth to the sixth centuries BCE

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.000
are their wisdom literature and take the form of dialogues between teachers

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.480
and students, reflecting about the origin, basis,

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.760
and support of the universe.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.020
What is the cause?

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.160
What is Brahman?

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.710
Whence are we born?

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.030
Whereby do we live?

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.760
On what are we established?

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.050
So asks the seeker in the Svetasvatara Upanishad.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:40.750
The teachers of the Upanishads point the way to a profound realization,

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:44.490
that atman, the innermost self or breath of life,

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:47.860
is identical with Brahman, the ultimate reality that

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.030
pervades the entire universe.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:54.260
Along with these texts of philosophical reflections, another set of texts

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:58.950
called the Dharmashastras were composed in the centuries before the common era.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:02.820
These focused on moral and social duties of individuals

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.560
based on their place in the society and their station in life.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.650
The philosophical reflections of the Upanishads

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:13.110
and the moral injunctions laid out in the Dharmashastras

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:17.980
were woven into two epics, the Ramayana and the Mahabharata.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.950
The mythological compendia called the Puranas, which

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:26.130
have been the core texts of Hindu since the early centuries of the common era,

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.970
contain hundreds of narratives about divine manifestations

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:33.030
and form the basis of a varied religious expressions

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.540
through ritual, pilgrimage, music, and dance.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.790
Numerous retellings and spin offs of the narratives contained in the epics

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:44.800
and the Puranas are also found in popular vernacular texts that

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.900
serve as scriptures for different groups,

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.880
especially those outside of elite communities.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.780
Daily worship often offered to images in a household shrine

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.580
might be directed to a particular god, goddess, or related figure

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:01.240
with whom an individual or family has a special affinity.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:05.660
Hinduism is closely identified with India, where more than 95%

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.400
of the world's Hindus live.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.910
In the millennia prior to widespread Euro-American contact and colonial

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.770
rule, religious identities throughout South Asia

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:19.209
tended to be particular, context sensitive, and somewhat fluid.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:22.870
Hindu, as an identity marker, grew more significant

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.650
as its scope both expanded and contracted during the struggle

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:28.610
for independence from Britain.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:34.630
The British partition of Bengal in 1905, and later of India in 1947, into Hindu

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:38.510
majority and Muslim majority areas generated a linking

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.060
of religious and national identities.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:45.130
However, there are Hindu communities in virtually every part of the world

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:46.680
today.

